  <body>
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="hero">
        <div class="hero-main panel">
          <div class="eyebrow">Unlisted SuperAda product brief · v1</div>
          <h1>Max AI Vertical Ops Assistant</h1>
          <p class="thesis">The wedge is not “Max AI for everyone.” Start with one service-business vertical, make the operator faster, then turn that trusted internal agent into the customer-facing layer.</p>
          <div class="pillrow">
            <span class="pill">Internal ops first</span><span class="pill">Customer agent second</span><span class="pill">Distribution beats ideas</span><span class="pill">B2B now, B2B2C later</span><span class="pill">Ugly pilot in weeks</span>
          </div>
          <div class="copybar"><button onclick="location.href='#next-steps'">Jump to next steps</button><button class="secondary" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save PDF</button></div>
        </div>
        <aside class="hero-side panel">
          <div class="metric"><b>3 models</b><span>B2C, B2B, and B2B2C were the core business-model paths discussed.</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>3 to 5</b><span>Warm businesses to pitch before overbuilding anything.</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>300</b><span>Approximate customer count mentioned for the in-home appliance repair phone-service operator.</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><b>$100/mo</b><span>Rough post-trial price anchor mentioned in the conversation.</span></div>
        </aside>
      </header>
    </div>
    <nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><a href="#problem">Problem</a><a href="#insight">Insight</a><a href="#customer">Customer</a><a href="#concept">Concept</a><a href="#business-model">Business Model</a><a href="#mvp">MVP</a><a href="#non-goals">Non-goals</a><a href="#risks">Risks</a><a href="#names">Names</a><a href="#next-steps">Next Steps</a></div></nav>
    <main class="wrap">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="callout panel"><span class="tag go">Recommendation</span><h3>Start in in-home appliance repair unless a warmer segment appears.</h3><p>The best clue in the call is not the abstract agent model. It is a possible distribution channel into a weird vertical with real back-office workflows.</p></div>
        <div class="callout panel"><span class="tag warn">Discipline</span><h3>Do not build the network product first.</h3><p>Earn trust as an internal operator assistant. The customer-facing Max AI layer should be an expansion, not the first sales promise.</p></div>
      </div>

      <section id="problem" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">01</div><h2>Problem</h2><p>Max AI has several plausible business models, but the broad consumer version is too expensive and distribution-heavy to start with. A direct-to-consumer agent needs integrations, consumer trust, and capital before there is proof that one use case works.</p><p>The opposite mistake is selling generic “agent infrastructure” to businesses. That is easier to sell, but it risks becoming a services wrapper with no network effect and no durable product wedge.</p><p>The practical problem is narrower: small service businesses already run on messy scheduling, phone, customer, and job systems. Owners and technicians need answers quickly: what is my next appointment, what happened with this customer, what parts do we need, who needs a follow-up, and what should I tell the customer?</p></section>

      <section id="insight" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">02</div><h2>Core Insight</h2><p>Distribution beats idea quality. Andy’s strongest point was execution: pick a vertical where there is already a relationship path, copy what works, launch quickly, and let usage decide.</p><p>The promising shape is B2B first, B2B2C later. Sell a tool that makes the business owner or technician better at their job. Once they trust it internally, expose the customer-facing side so customers can ask status, booking, availability, pricing basics, and job questions through channels they already use.</p><p>This is the Shopify / Shop analogy from the call: Shopify starts as merchant infrastructure, then Shop becomes the consumer surface. Max AI can start as service-business ops infrastructure, then become the customer agent network across those businesses.</p></section>

      <section id="customer" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">03</div><h2>Customer, Buyer, User</h2><ul><li><strong>Initial buyer:</strong> owner/operator of a small service business or a vertical distributor who already sells tools into that niche.</li><li><strong>Initial user:</strong> technician, dispatcher, or owner asking operational questions during the workday.</li><li><strong>Later user:</strong> end customer asking for status, booking, reminders, availability, and basic pricing through WhatsApp, iMessage, SMS, or web chat.</li><li><strong>Initial wedge:</strong> in-home appliance repair, because the call surfaced a possible connector with deep access to that market and the tools those operators use.</li></ul></section>

      <section id="concept" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">04</div><h2>Product Concept</h2><p>Build an internal AI ops assistant for one service vertical. It connects to the systems the business already uses: scheduling, customer management, job records, parts notes, phone service, and message history. It answers operational questions, drafts follow-ups, and helps the worker get through the day faster.</p><p>The first product should feel less like a chatbot and more like a workbench. The assistant knows the next job, the customer context, the likely parts, the open follow-up, and the status of recent work. The customer-facing layer comes later, using the same infrastructure.</p></section>

      <section id="business-model" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">05</div><h2>Business Model Options</h2><ol><li><strong>B2C:</strong> sell Max AI directly to consumers. Biggest long-term upside, but hardest distribution and highest capital need.</li><li><strong>B2B:</strong> sell agent infrastructure to businesses. Most practical starting motion, but weaker network effect if customers never know Max AI exists.</li><li><strong>B2B2C:</strong> business pays, customers interact with a Max AI surface. Best long-term model because consumers can reuse the same assistant across businesses.</li></ol><p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> start B2B in one vertical, design the architecture and brand so it can become B2B2C when the first vertical proves daily value.</p></section>

      <section id="mvp" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">06</div><h2>MVP</h2><p>The MVP should be embarrassingly narrow. One vertical, one business, one workflow, one measurable pain.</p><h3>Version 0.1 workflow</h3><ul><li>Connect to scheduling and customer/job data.</li><li>Let an operator ask: “What is my next appointment?”</li><li>Let an operator ask: “What parts do I need to order?”</li><li>Let an operator ask for job/customer context before a visit.</li><li>Draft follow-up reminders or status messages for customers.</li><li>Log what the agent did so the owner trusts it.</li></ul><h3>Commercial test</h3><ul><li>Offer the first 60 to 90 days free to a tiny pilot cohort.</li><li>Ask for a card only after repeated usage.</li><li>Use roughly $100/month as the first price anchor, subject to real willingness to pay.</li></ul></section>

      <section id="non-goals" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">07</div><h2>Non-goals</h2><ul><li>No “Max AI for everyone” launch before a vertical proves usage.</li><li>No generic agent-platform deck as the first sales artifact.</li><li>No marketplace/network build before business-side value is proven.</li><li>No promise that every service vertical works the same way.</li><li>No customer-facing bot until the operator trusts the internal assistant.</li></ul></section>

      <section id="risks" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">08</div><h2>Risks and Falsifiers</h2><ul><li><strong>Distribution risk:</strong> the appliance-repair connector may not introduce anyone, or the vertical may be too relationship-protected.</li><li><strong>Workflow risk:</strong> the systems may be too fragmented for a quick useful integration.</li><li><strong>Value risk:</strong> operators may not use an assistant daily, even if they like the demo.</li><li><strong>Buyer risk:</strong> $100/month may be too low to support the work or too high for the perceived value.</li><li><strong>Network-effect risk:</strong> the product may remain vertical B2B software and never earn a consumer surface.</li><li><strong>Falsifier:</strong> if 3 to 5 warm businesses will not trial an internal assistant for free, the wedge is weaker than it sounds.</li><li><strong>Falsifier:</strong> if technicians do not ask repeat questions after week one, the internal ops assistant is not yet solving a painful enough workflow.</li></ul></section>

      <section id="names" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">09</div><h2>10 Name Ideas</h2><ol class="names">{names.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}</ol></section>

      <section id="next-steps" class="panel section-card"><div class="kicker">10</div><h2>Next Steps</h2><ol>{nextSteps.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}</ol></section>

      <footer class="footer">Source: 2026-06-20 Andy / Henry voice memo transcribed with Parakeet. Writing route used: main OpenClaw model after the delegated Opus publish pass lost execution context. This page is intentionally unlisted and noindex.</footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
